Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Core diagnosis from all attempts:
// 1. The model generates symbolic variable names like {n0}, {r}, {d} inside << >> spans
//    instead of actual numeric expressions. This is the root cause of 90%+ syntax failures.
// 2. The arithmetic parser rejects these variable-name tokens entirely.
// 3. Attempts 9 (best) works by detecting natural "<<" emissions and constraining the content.
//    But 57.1% syntax means many spans still fail because the model insists on using variables.
// 4. Key insight: We need MULTIPLE chances at valid constrained spans. The evaluator extracts
//    the LAST complete span. If early spans fail (because the model reasons symbolically),
//    we need the model to eventually emit a numeric answer.
// 5. NEW STRATEGY: 
//    a. During unconstrained generation, detect "<<" and enter constrained mode.
//    b. INSIDE constrained mode, if we see the model is trying to use variable tokens (which
//       the parser rejects), we need to keep trying with constrained steps.
//    c. Key fix: Use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep inside constrained spans to discourage repeating
//       variable tokens that were rejected.
//    d. When a span produces EOS (parser stuck), instead of just breaking, do a rollback and
//       open a NEW constrained span to try again.
// 6. The prompt must be crystal clear: the content inside << >> must be ONLY numbers.
// 7. Critical new approach: After RollbackConstrainedToComplete + close, we don't break but
//    continue generating. This lets the model try again with a fresh span.
// 8. Also: after EOS in constrained mode, instead of breaking, rollback + continue. 
//    The model may self-correct and emit another << >> with proper content.
// 9. One span structure: avoid letting model re-enter free generation after a broken span.
//    Instead, force a new constrained span right after a failed/empty one to get valid content.
// 10. Actually the problem might be simpler: AdaptiveConstrainedStep uses a hard mask so
//     variable tokens like "{n0}" cannot be selected. But then why do we get syntax failures?
//     Answer: The constrained step selects valid tokens, but the model runs out of valid tokens
//     quickly (EOS fires) and we break. The span content is too short or empty.
// 11. Revised insight: The spans we generate ARE syntactically valid (when we actually constrain
//     them), but the evaluator also sees the UNCONSTRAINED spans the model generates BEFORE
//     our constrained intervention. Those unconstrained spans contain {variables} and fail.
// 12. Solution: We need to PREVENT the model from emitting unconstrained "<<" spans with
//     variable content. Do this by ALWAYS constraining when inside a span.
//     The current approach already does this, but the problem is:
//     - Model emits "<<" which we detect
//     - We enter constrained mode
//     - Constrained step generates valid first tokens of an expression
//     - But the model wants to output something like "n0 * r" which the parser may reject
//     - We keep selecting parser-valid tokens until EOS
//     - The result is a valid arithmetic expression but possibly wrong semantics
// 13. THE REAL INSIGHT: The evaluator looks at visible spans. All spans get tested.
//     Spans the model emits before the "<<" we detect in our loop... wait.
//     Actually, with stepTokenBudget=1, every token goes through our loop ONE AT A TIME.
//     So we ALWAYS see every "<<". The unconstrained spans happen because:
//     a. We detect "<<", enter constrained mode
//     b. The first constrained token we pick is valid (e.g., a number)
//     c. But then the NEXT token might be EOS (model wants to bail)
//     d. We rollback and exit constrained mode
//     e. But the "<<" token is still in generated! And we exited constrained mode.
//     f. The evaluator sees <<[valid_prefix]>> but the content is malformed.
// 14. FIX: After rolling back inside constrained mode, we should ALSO remove the "<<" from
//     generated. But we can't easily do this. 
//     Alternative: After rollback to complete, CLOSE the span (even if content is empty/short).
//     OR: When we exit constrained mode after EOS/rollback, continue to the CloseSpanIfComplete
//     to emit a proper ">>".
// 15. BETTER FIX: When EOS occurs inside constrained span:
//     a. RollbackConstrainedToComplete - get best complete prefix
//     b. If it's non-empty, close the span (emit ">>") and continue
//     c. If it's empty, we need to either: force a minimal valid expression OR
//        somehow remove the "<<" from generated (not possible without custom helpers)
//     d. For empty rollback: just force-close with empty content... but that's invalid
//     e. Actually: force at least ONE valid token before closing
// 16. CONCRETE NEW STRATEGY:
//     - Free generation until "<<" detected
//     - Enter constrained mode  
//     - Generate constrained tokens until COMPLETE (CloseSpanIfComplete fires) or EOS
//     - If EOS: rollback to best complete prefix; if non-empty close span; if empty use
//       a single forced valid token approach
//     - After span closed, continue free generation (more spans can be generated)
//     - Never break early if we can recover
// 17. The key improvement over attempt 9 (best): Ensure EVERY opened span gets properly closed.
//     In attempt 9, EOS breaks the loop leaving an open span. Fix: after EOS in constrained
//     mode, rollback + close + continue (don't break).
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
//   - Unconstrained branch (next != "<<"): insideConstrainedOut stays false, vacuously true.
//   - Unconstrained branch (next == "<<"): EnterObservedConstrainedSpan sets currentConstrainedOut
//     := [], and parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds by method precondition. Invariant holds.
//   - EOS in unconstrained branch: break, invariant holds at loop exit.
//   - Constrained branch, CloseSpanIfComplete (closed=true): insideConstrainedOut becomes false,
//     implication vacuously satisfied; currentConstrainedOut := [].
//   - Constrained branch, CloseSpanIfComplete (closed=false): state unchanged, invariant holds.
//   - Constrained branch, AdaptiveConstrainedStep returns non-EOS: AppendConstrainedToken
//     extends currentConstrainedOut with a valid next token, preserving IsValidPrefix.
//   - Constrained branch, EOS from AdaptiveConstrainedStep: RollbackConstrainedToComplete returns
//     a complete-or-empty prefix that satisfies IsValidPrefix; then if we close via
//     CloseSpanIfComplete the span closes cleanly; if result empty, we set insideConstrainedOut
//     := false making implication vacuous.
//
// progress: |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
//   - Each branch increments steps by exactly the number of step-consuming helpers called.
//   - Unconstrained UnconstrainedStep: steps+1, generated grows by 0 (EOS) or 1. OK.
//   - EnterObservedConstrainedSpan: steps+0, generated unchanged. OK.
//   - CloseSpanIfComplete: steps+1, generated grows by at most 1 (">>" if closed). OK.
//   - AdaptiveConstrainedStep: steps+1, generated grows by at most 1 via AppendConstrainedToken.
//     EOS path: steps+1, RollbackConstrainedToComplete may shrink generated. OK (shrink is safe).
//   - After EOS rollback: if we then call CloseSpanIfComplete that costs steps+1 and grows
//     generated by at most 1. We only call this if steps < maxSteps. OK.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

helpers.AppendTaskGuidance(lm, "Solve this math word problem step by step. At the end, output your numeric answer inside << >>. The content inside << >> must be ONLY a numeric arithmetic expression with actual digits and operators (+, -, *, /, (, )). For example: <<42>> or <<(3 + 5) * 2>>. Never put variable names, letters, or words inside << >>.");

var steps: nat := 0;

while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
{
  if !insideConstrainedOut {
    var next := helpers.UnconstrainedStep(lm, prompt, generated);
    steps := steps + 1;
    if next == eosToken {
      break;
    } else {
      generated := generated + [next];
      if next == "<<" {
        var og, oi, oc := helpers.EnterObservedConstrainedSpan(lm, generated);
        generated := og;
        insideConstrainedOut := oi;
        currentConstrainedOut := oc;
      }
    }
  } else {
    // Inside constrained span - check if already complete
    var cg, ci, cc, closed := helpers.CloseSpanIfComplete(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
    steps := steps + 1;
    if closed {
      generated := cg;
      insideConstrainedOut := ci;
      currentConstrainedOut := cc;
    } else {
      // Not complete yet - generate next constrained token
      if steps >= maxSteps {
        // No budget left - rollback and force close or exit
        var rg, rc := helpers.RollbackConstrainedToComplete(parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
        generated := rg;
        currentConstrainedOut := rc;
        if |rc| == 0 {
          insideConstrainedOut := false;
          currentConstrainedOut := [];
        }
        // If rc is non-empty and complete, we'll close next iteration, but no budget - just exit
        insideConstrainedOut := false;
        currentConstrainedOut := [];
        break;
      }
      var constrainedPrompt := prompt + generated[..|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|];
      var next := helpers.AdaptiveConstrainedStep(lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, validTokenGroups, 4.0, 12, eosToken);
      steps := steps + 1;
      if next == eosToken {
        // EOS inside constrained span - rollback to best complete prefix
        var rg, rc := helpers.RollbackConstrainedToComplete(parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
        generated := rg;
        currentConstrainedOut := rc;
        // Now try to close the span if it's complete (it should be after rollback to complete)
        if steps < maxSteps {
          var cg2, ci2, cc2, closed2 := helpers.CloseSpanIfComplete(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
          steps := steps + 1;
          if closed2 {
            generated := cg2;
            insideConstrainedOut := ci2;
            currentConstrainedOut := cc2;
          } else {
            // Couldn't close - just exit constrained mode
            insideConstrainedOut := false;
            currentConstrainedOut := [];
          }
        } else {
          insideConstrainedOut := false;
          currentConstrainedOut := [];
        }
        // After handling the span (whether closed or not), continue generation
        // Don't break - let model continue and possibly emit another span
      } else {
        var ag, ai, ac := helpers.AppendConstrainedToken(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut, next);
        generated := ag;
        insideConstrainedOut := ai;
        currentConstrainedOut := ac;
      }
    }
  }
}

cost := steps;

